</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Fondazione Bruno Kessler, Italy</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Textual prompt tuning adapts Vision-Language Models (e.g., CLIP)
in federated learning by tuning lightweight input tokens (or prompts) on local client data, while keeping network weights frozen.
Post training, only the prompts are shared by the clients with the central server for aggregation. However, textual prompt tuning often struggles with overfitting to known concepts and may be overly reliant on memorized text features, limiting its adaptability to unseen concepts.
To address this limitation, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fed</span>erated <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>ultimodal <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V</span>isual <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>rompt Tuning (FedMVP) that conditions the prompts on comprehensive contextual information – image-conditioned features and textual attribute features of a class – that is multimodal in nature. At the core of FedMVP is a PromptFormer module that synergistically aligns textual and visual features through cross-attention, enabling richer contexual integration. The dynamically generated multimodal visual prompts are then input to the frozen vision encoder of CLIP, and trained with a combination of CLIP similarity loss and a consistency loss. Extensive evaluation on 20 datasets spanning three generalization settings demonstrates that FedMVP not only preserves performance on in-distribution classes and domains, but also displays higher generalizability to unseen classes and domains when compared to state-of-the-art methods. Codes will be released upon acceptance.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2504.20860v1/teaser_mvp.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/397;" width="685" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of federated prompt tuning methods<span id="S1.F1.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: (a) a textual prompt tuning method (<em id="S1.F1.11.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.F1.11.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, FedTPG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>) encodes </span>unimodal<span id="S1.F1.11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> contextual information, <em id="S1.F1.11.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S1.F1.11.2.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, class names, in the learnable prompts, (b) our proposed method FedMVP injects </span>multimodal<span id="S1.F1.11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> contextual information – image and attributes – into the prompts.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A successful training paradigm involves training neural networks on large, centralized datasets using powerful GPUs, enabling models to learn robust and generalizable representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. However, practical constraints often prevent centralized data aggregation, <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, due to privacy regulations, creating a critical need for decentralized training frameworks that achieve high performance without central data access. Federated Learning (FL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> has emerged as a promising solution, enabling multiple clients to collaboratively train a global model without requiring direct data sharing. In FL, clients train models locally and transmit only model updates (<span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, gradients or parameters) to a central server, which aggregates these updates to improve the global model. The research in FL is primarily aimed at: reducing communication rounds between clients and server <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>, faster and lightweight training algorithms on clients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> and improving generalization while exhibiting high data heterogeneity across clients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Pretrained Vision-Language Models (VLMs), such as CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, have emerged as promising candidates for FL due to their strong generalization capabilities and robustness to distribution shifts.
However, the high communication and computational demands of parameter-heavy VLMs poses challenges for FL, especially in bandwidth- or compute-constrained environments (<em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, robots). To make CLIP viable for the FL setting, prompt tuning (<em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text"></span>, CoOp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>, VPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>) has been adopted as a preferred technique that appends lightweight learnable tokens (or <span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prompts</span>) to the input tokens of the CLIP image and/or text encoder. Each client trains the prompts on the local data, while keeping the CLIP backbone frozen, and then dispatches these prompts to the server, where they are updated through aggregation and sent back. This speeds up client training as each client needs access to only a few samples per class (<em id="S1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text"></span>, 16/class <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>), and reduces communication overhead as only the lightweight prompts (<em id="S1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text"></span>, 0.37% of the whole network) need to be communicated, instead of the entire network.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Although prompt tuning has proven to be effective in the FL setting, it suffers from reduced generalization to unseen classes and unseen domains – a typical heterogeneous setup native to non independent and identically distributed (non-i.i.d.) FL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> – due to severe overfitting on the local training data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>. It is attributed to the fact that prompts learn a <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">static context</span> from a set of seen classes, which being fixed once learned, fail to capture the generalizable elements spread across clients. To circumvent the issue of reduced generalization, recent CLIP-based FL methods condition the <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">textual prompts</span> either on the class information, as in FedTPG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> (see Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>a), or on visual information, as in FedCoCoOp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>. This auxiliary conditioning on the input, or <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contextual information</span>, makes the prompts <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dynamic</span> and thus offers a better generalization to unseen distributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we argue that the FL setup being highly heterogeneous, where clients have data from disjoint classes and domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, it is not sufficient to solely exploit either of the two modalities for contextual information. Rather, FL demands more comprehensive contextual information that encompasses all the available modalities to condition input prompts. To this end, we propose <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fed</span>erated <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>ultimodal <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V</span>isual <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>rompt Tuning (FedMVP) that leverages <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multimodal contextual information</span> to condition the prompts (see Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>b), which comprise: (i) <span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">visual</span> features of the input image itself, and (ii) <span id="S1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">textual</span> features of attributes of a class (<em id="S1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text"></span>, “two legs”, “beak”, etc. for the class “hen”). Our key intuition is that (ii) promotes learning transferable prompts for unseen classes that share attributes similar to seen classes (<em id="S1.p4.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p4.1.11" class="ltx_text"></span>, unseen class “seagull” will share some attributes with the seen class “hen”), and (i) promotes transferability for unseen classes and domains where attributes cannot be described via text (<em id="S1.p4.1.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.p4.1.13" class="ltx_text"></span>, texture or abstract concepts). In a nutshell, the dual conditioning offers richer contextual information to the prompts, which helps in generalizing to classes or domains that share similar properties.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S1.T1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.T1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Summary of federated prompt-tuning methods<span id="S1.T1.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. (<span id="S1.T1.11.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span>) indicates the learnable prompts are initialized randomly, (<span id="S1.T1.11.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">✓</span>) signifies learnable prompts are conditioned on contextual information (<span id="S1.T1.11.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, textual or visual inputs). <span id="S1.T1.11.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prompting</span> column denotes the CLIP encoder wherein learnable prompts are given as input (<em id="S1.T1.11.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S1.T1.11.1.6" class="ltx_text"></span>, “Textual” means the text encoder of CLIP takes prompts as input).</span></span></figcaption>
<div id="S1.T1.12" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:269.8pt;height:78pt;vertical-align:-37.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-33.7pt,9.8pt) scale(0.8,0.8) ;">
<table id="S1.T1.12.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S1.T1.12.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Method</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" colspan="2">Contextual information</td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text">Prompting</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.12.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Textual</td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Visual</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.12.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">FedKgCoOp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Textual</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.12.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">FedVPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Visual</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.12.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">FedTPG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Textual</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.12.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">FedCoCoOp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Textual</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.12.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">FedMaPLe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.7.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.7.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Multimodal</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.12.1.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">FedMVP (Ours)</td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.8.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b"><span id="S1.T1.12.1.8.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.12.1.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">Visual</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In detail, the proposed FedMVP framework contains a <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PromptFormer</span> network (see Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>b) that synergistically merges the information from visual and textual modalities with cross-attention mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> to generate the prompts. The resulting <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multimodal prompts</span> are then injected at the visual space of the frozen vision encoder of CLIP, unlike the existing methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> that inject prompts through text encoder. The key differences between our FedMVP and existing CLIP-based FL methods have been summarized in Tab. <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The PromptFormer is trained in an end-to-end manner with the CLIP loss and a consistency loss. Once trained, each participating client shares the lightweight PromptFormer network parameters with the server for aggregation through FedAvg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and thus enabling knowledge sharing among clients. During inference, the cross-attention learned by the PromptFormer enables the model to dynamically adjust the prompts, enhancing generalizability to unseen classes and domains. We evaluated FedMVP on three generalization settings encompassing 20 datasets that measure generalization to unseen classes, domains, and combinations of them. Experiments demonstrate that FedMVP consistently outperforms the state-of-the-art CLIP-based FL methods by 1.57%-2.26%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our main <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">contributions</span> are: (<span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">i</span>) We highlight the importance of leveraging <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">multimodal contextual information</span> in prompt tuning based FL setup in order to alleviate the issue of reduced generalizability to unseen data. (<span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ii</span>) We propose FedMVP that synergistically combines the visual features of the image and textual features of the attributes of classes to create <span id="S1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">multimodal</span> visual prompts. This provides richer contextual information to the model and higher transferability to unseen classes and domains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt Tuning of VLMs.</span> VLMs such as CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, ALIGN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, and Florence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> leverage large image-text datasets to enable multimodal understanding, performing exceptionally well in open-vocabulary tasks. Nonetheless, fully fine-tuning VLMs for specialized tasks (<em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, long tailed recognition) without sacrificing their ability to generalize remains a challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Prompt tuning approaches have been proposed for parameter efficient and effective adaptation that introduce a set of continuous learnable tokens (or prompts) onto the frozen CLIP backbone. Prompt tuning approaches can be broadly categorized into three categories depending on the manner in which CLIP is prompted: (i) <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">textual prompting</span> that inject prompts in the input space of the text encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, (ii) <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">visual prompting</span> that injects prompts in the input space of the vision encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, and (iii) <span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multimodal prompting</span> that injects prompts in both the text and vision encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Closely related to our work, MaPLe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> that injects prompts to both text and vision encoder is not suitable for memory and compute efficient FL since it requires parts of both the encoders to be unlocked during tuning, risking overfitting. Moreover, it does not allow for image-conditioned dynamic prompting, which has shown to be effective in FL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>. On the other hand, our proposed FedMVP enables multimodal prompting by involving only the vision encoder and supports instance-specific prompting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Federated Learning of VLMs.</span> Prompt tuning has emerged as an effective technique for FL of VLMs since it allows clients to collaboratively train on a heterogeneously distributed dataset and adapt faster with a fewer samples per class. Prompts being lightweight lends well to the FL setup as it reduces communication overhead. Owing to the benefits offered by prompt tuning, several works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> have adapted prompt tuning approaches to the federated setup.
For instance,
PromptFL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> extended CoOp to FL via textual prompting, enhancing the generalization performance of client-server interactions in vision tasks. Wherever, FedCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> learns a lightweight adapter in the client models, similar to CLIP-Adapter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, and share their parameters to the server.
FedOTP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> introduced global and local prompts, optimizing shared parameters through optimal transport, while DiPrompt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> studied multi domain learning in the federated setting by allowing multiple domains per client. Additionally, FedCola <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> proposed to use multimodal transformers, assuming clients have access to different data modalities. The closest work to ours is FedTPG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, where a prompt generation network is learned across multiple clients. Additionally, this network leverages textual inputs, enhancing its ability to generalize to unseen classes. Our work significantly differs from previous methods in two key ways: (i) we exploit class attributes to further enhance generalization capabilities, and (ii) we leverage multimodal prompts from the proposed PromptFormer for visual prompt tuning, instead of textual prompt tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classification via textual descriptions.</span> Classification accuracy of CLIP on downstream tasks or open-vocabulary datasets is sensitive to the quality of text prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. Several works have explored this space by using simple handcrafted prompts (<em id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, “a photo of a [<span id="S2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CLS</span>]”) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> or via augmenting the handcrafted prompts with richer attributes obtained from LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. There also exists a family of methods, such as LaCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, LaBo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> and VFC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, that have proposed to further refine CLIP weights with enriched captions from LLMs for improved performance. Acknowledging the importance of class attributes for improved generalization to unseen concepts and domains, an important desideratum in non-IID federated set up, we, for the first time in FL, introduce LLM generated class attributes for enriching the image-conditioned visual prompts through the PromptFormer network.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, first we introduce the non-IID federated learning (FL) setup in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Problem formulation ‣ 3 Method ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, followed by introducing preliminaries on prompt tuning for FL in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Preliminaries on Prompt Tuning ‣ 3 Method ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Finally, in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Federated Multimodal Visual Prompt Tuning ‣ 3 Method ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> we introduce our proposed solution for FL.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2504.20860v1/pipeline.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/276;" width="685" height="276" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of proposed Federated Multimodal Visual Prompt Tuning<span id="S3.F2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (</span>FedMVP<span id="S3.F2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">). Each client <math id="S3.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> trains the parameters of the PromptFormer network <math id="S3.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and multi-head self-attention on its local data. At each round, the server aggregates the trainable parameters from the clients and sends the updated parameters back to the client. The PromptFormer uses cross-attention mechanism to fuse the visual and textual attribute feature to generate multimodal visual prompts <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are then used for visual prompt tuning. </span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Problem formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a FL setting that is characterized by multiple remote clients running stochastic gradient descent (SGD) on locally available data, and a central server that aggregates models from multiple remote clients. More formally, let each remote client <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> has access to a dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{i}=\{\bm{\mathrm{x}}_{i,j},y_{i,j}\}^{n_{i}}_{j=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{i}=\{\bm{\mathrm{x}}_{i,j},y_{i,j}\}^{n_{i}}_{j=1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{x}}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{x}}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\textsuperscript{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext><sup class="ltx_sup">th</sup></mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\textsuperscript{th}</annotation></semantics></math> image from the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\textsuperscript{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext><sup class="ltx_sup">th</sup></mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\textsuperscript{th}</annotation></semantics></math> client, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding class label and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of samples in client <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Let the set of text class names in each client be denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}=\{c_{i,k}\}^{K_{i}}_{k=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}=\{c_{i,k}\}^{K_{i}}_{k=1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of classes in client <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. As in FedTPG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, we follow a non-IID federated setup, where the samples in each client are drawn from a disjoint set of classes, <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}\cap\mathcal{C}_{l}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}\cap\mathcal{C}_{l}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. As an example, the training data on each mobile device typically depends on the user, which might not be representative of the general population <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Under such a federated setting, we investigate two kinds of non-IID federated setups, where, besides the in-distribution performance, we also test the generalization ability of the aggregated server model to (i) <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">classes</span> unseen during training, and (ii) <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">domains</span> or <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">datasets</span> unseen during training. The need for generalization to unseen classes and domains makes this setup more challenging than the traditional setting in the FL literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Preliminaries on Prompt Tuning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Textual prompt tuning (TPT), <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, CoOp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>, has emerged as an efficient technique in CLIP-based FL that keeps the CLIP backbone frozen and locally learns lightweight prompt vectors on each client. The locally learned prompts are then communicated to the server, as in PromptFL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. Specifically, the handcrafted text prompts (<em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g</em>.<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text"></span>, <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“a photo of a</span> <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[CLS]</span>”) input to the text encoder <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is augmented with the learnable soft prompts <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},v_{2},\dots,v_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},v_{2},\dots,v_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, as shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, TPT can often lead to overfitting and reduced generalization due to the static context learned on the seen classes. This can be exacerbated in the non-IID FL setup due to the high data heterogeneity among the clients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, visual prompt tuning (VPT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> adapts pre-trained vision encoder <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> by injecting small number of visual learnable parameters into ViT’s input space. More formally, let an input image <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> be divided in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> patches, yielding a collection of patch embeddings (augmented with positional encodings) at the input of the vision encoder as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{E}}\in\mathbb{R}^{b\times d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐄</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{E}}\in\mathbb{R}^{b\times d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding dimension. A [<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CLS</span>] token <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is concatenated with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>, encapsulating the initial input representation for the ViT layers of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. VPT further augments the input by concatenating a set of randomly initialized learnable visual prompts <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{P}}=\{p_{1},p_{2},\dots,p_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{P}}=\{p_{1},p_{2},\dots,p_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is the prompt length. The first layer input of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is then redefined as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{I}}=[\bm{\mathrm{z}};\bm{\mathrm{E}};\bm{\mathrm{P}}]\in\mathbb{R}^{(1+b+m)\times d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝐄</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝐏</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{I}}=[\bm{\mathrm{z}};\bm{\mathrm{E}};\bm{\mathrm{P}}]\in\mathbb{R}^{(1+b+m)\times d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where “;” represents concatenation. Although VPT enables more nuanced understanding of the visual space, it suffers from the same issues as TPT, <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, limited generalization, as demonstrated later in Sec. <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Federated Multimodal Visual Prompt Tuning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our proposed method FedMVP deviates from the TPT and VPT paradigms, and incorporates <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">multimodal contextual information</span> from both the visual and textual modalities to improve generalization in the FL setup.
As shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Method ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, at the core of our novel architecture is a prompt generator, called <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PromptFormer</span>, that conditions the visual prompt generation on the task-related information: input image and text attributes corresponding to the class names, to obtain <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than randomly initializing them. This makes <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">context-dependant</span>, assisting in generalization to unseen classes and domains. Once trained on each client, only the parameters of the lightweight PromptFormer network is communicated to the server, and thus, keeping the communication overhead low.
Next, we describe visual token generation with PromptFormer (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3.SSS1" title="3.3.1 Visual Token Generation with PromptFormer ‣ 3.3 Federated Multimodal Visual Prompt Tuning ‣ 3 Method ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3.1</span></a>), training losses in FedMVP (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3.SSS2" title="3.3.2 Training of FedMVP ‣ 3.3 Federated Multimodal Visual Prompt Tuning ‣ 3 Method ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3.2</span></a>) and how we conduct client-side training and server aggregation (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3.SSS3" title="3.3.3 Client-side training and server aggregation ‣ 3.3 Federated Multimodal Visual Prompt Tuning ‣ 3 Method ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Visual Token Generation with PromptFormer</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce the PromptFormer network <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterized by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>, designed to generate contextually rich visual tokens (or prompts) for each client <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> by leveraging both attribute features and visual patch embeddings (see Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Method ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
Concretely, we use the CLIP text encoder <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to extract embeddings <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{A}}_{i}=\{\mathcal{E}_{t}(\texttt{LLM}(c_{k}))\}^{K}_{k=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{A}}_{i}=\{\mathcal{E}_{t}(\texttt{LLM}(c_{k}))\}^{K}_{k=1}</annotation></semantics></math> of the text attributes<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              For example, for the class name “giraffe” the LLM outputs “Exceptionally long neck, unique coat pattern with irregular brown patches, …”</span></span></span> <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the text class names <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, generated using a large lanuage model (LLM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> (see Supp. Mat. for Attribute Generation process). Going forward, we drop the client index <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> for notational clarity. The PromptFormer then takes as input the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> visual patch embeddings <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to an image <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and the text embeddings <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate a set of enriched <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> multimodal visual prompts <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{P}}\in\mathrm{R}^{m\times d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{P}}\in\mathrm{R}^{m\times d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{P}}=\{p_{j}\}^{m}_{j=1}=f_{\Theta}(\bm{\mathrm{A}},\bm{\mathrm{E}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{P}}=\{p_{j}\}^{m}_{j=1}=f_{\Theta}(\bm{\mathrm{A}},\bm{\mathrm{E}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consequently, the input to the first layer of the visual encoder <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is redefined as <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\bm{\mathrm{z}};\bm{\mathrm{E}};\bm{\mathrm{P}}]\in\mathbb{R}^{(1+b+m)\times d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝐄</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝐏</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\bm{\mathrm{z}};\bm{\mathrm{E}};\bm{\mathrm{P}}]\in\mathbb{R}^{(1+b+m)\times d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Intuitively, multimodal visual prompts carry richer contextual information, and helps the network in generalizing to unseen classes and domains that share commonalities.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Architecture of PromptFormer.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The PromptFormer architecture comprises: lightweight multi-head cross-attention (MHCA) modules, feed-forward network (FFN) and a linear projection layer; transforming multimodal features into comprehensive visual prompts.
In detail, the visual patch embedding <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> interacts with the linearly projected attribute features <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime}=T_{\text{proj}}(\bm{\mathrm{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>proj</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime}=T_{\text{proj}}(\bm{\mathrm{A}})</annotation></semantics></math> to get multimodal visual prompts as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{c}\bm{\mathrm{P}}(\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime},\bm{\mathrm{E}})=\text{FFN}(\text{CrossAttention}(\bm{\mathrm{Q}}_{\bm{\mathrm{E}}},\bm{\mathrm{K}}_{\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{{}^{\prime}}},\bm{\mathrm{V}}_{\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{{}^{\prime}}}));\\[5.0pt]
\text{with}\;\bm{\mathrm{Q}}_{\bm{\mathrm{E}}}=\bm{\mathrm{E}}W_{\bm{\mathrm{Q}}},\bm{\mathrm{K}}_{\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{{}^{\prime}}}=\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime}W_{\bm{\mathrm{K}}},\bm{\mathrm{V}}_{\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{{}^{\prime}}}=\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime}W_{\bm{\mathrm{V}}},\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐄</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mtext>FFN</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>CrossAttention</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐄</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐊</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext>with</mtext><mi>𝐐</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐄</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐄</mi><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐊</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐊</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐕</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{c}\bm{\mathrm{P}}(\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime},\bm{\mathrm{E}})=\text{FFN}(\text{CrossAttention}(\bm{\mathrm{Q}}_{\bm{\mathrm{E}}},\bm{\mathrm{K}}_{\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{{}^{\prime}}},\bm{\mathrm{V}}_{\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{{}^{\prime}}}));\\[5.0pt]
\text{with}\;\bm{\mathrm{Q}}_{\bm{\mathrm{E}}}=\bm{\mathrm{E}}W_{\bm{\mathrm{Q}}},\bm{\mathrm{K}}_{\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{{}^{\prime}}}=\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime}W_{\bm{\mathrm{K}}},\bm{\mathrm{V}}_{\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{{}^{\prime}}}=\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime}W_{\bm{\mathrm{V}}},\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> are transformed into query vectors <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{Q}}_{\bm{\mathrm{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>𝐄</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{Q}}_{\bm{\mathrm{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math> using query matrices <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\bm{\mathrm{Q}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\bm{\mathrm{Q}}}</annotation></semantics></math>; and the projected attribute features <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are transformed into key and value vectors <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{K}}_{\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{K}}_{\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{V}}_{\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{V}}_{\bm{\mathrm{A}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, using key and value matrices <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\bm{\mathrm{K}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>𝐊</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\bm{\mathrm{K}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\bm{\mathrm{V}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>𝐕</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\bm{\mathrm{V}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, through the cross-attention mechanism, the visual features learn to attend to the relevant parts of the corresponding attribute features. For instance, the patches depicting the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">legs</span> of the class “dog” will learn to attend to the attributes “four legged”. When an unseen class comprise an unseen animal with four legs, the final prompts <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> will contain this relevant information derived from the attributes “four legged”. Additional details of PromptFormer architecture are reported in the Supp. Mat.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2 </span>Training of FedMVP</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After the visual prompts are obtained with PromptFormer as per Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Architecture of PromptFormer. ‣ 3.3.1 Visual Token Generation with PromptFormer ‣ 3.3 Federated Multimodal Visual Prompt Tuning ‣ 3 Method ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we concatenate the [<span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CLS</span>] token, the patch embeddings, and the visual prompts to get <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{I}}=[\bm{\mathrm{z}};\bm{\mathrm{E}};\bm{\mathrm{P}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝐄</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝐏</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{I}}=[\bm{\mathrm{z}};\bm{\mathrm{E}};\bm{\mathrm{P}}]</annotation></semantics></math> following Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Preliminaries on Prompt Tuning ‣ 3 Method ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, and feed it to
the visual encoder <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Collectively, the parameters of PromptFormer <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\{\Theta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\{\Theta\}</annotation></semantics></math> are the only trainable parameters of FedMVP, and the rest of the weights are kept frozen.
To train the model we employ the CLIP cross-entropy loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{\text{ce}}=-\underset{(\bm{\mathrm{x}},y)\sim\mathcal{D}}{\mathbb{E}}y\log p(y|\bm{\mathrm{I}}),\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ce</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">y</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{\text{ce}}=-\underset{(\bm{\mathrm{x}},y)\sim\mathcal{D}}{\mathbb{E}}y\log p(y|\bm{\mathrm{I}}),\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the ground truth label, and the prediction probability of CLIP between the visual features <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{v}}=\mathcal{E}_{v}(\bm{\mathrm{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{v}}=\mathcal{E}_{v}(\bm{\mathrm{I}})</annotation></semantics></math> and text features <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{t}}_{k}=\mathcal{E}_{t}([\text{A photo of [CLASS]};\texttt{LLM}(c_{k})])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mtext>A photo of [CLASS]</mtext><mo>;</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">LLM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{t}}_{k}=\mathcal{E}_{t}([\text{A photo of [CLASS]};\texttt{LLM}(c_{k})])</annotation></semantics></math> for class <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y=k|\bm{\mathrm{I}})=\frac{\exp(\text{cos}(\bm{\mathrm{v}},\bm{\mathrm{t}}_{k}))/\tau)}{\sum^{K}_{k^{\prime}=1}\exp(\text{cos}(\bm{\mathrm{v}},\bm{\mathrm{t}}_{k^{\prime}}))/\tau)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y=k|\bm{\mathrm{I}})=\frac{\exp(\text{cos}(\bm{\mathrm{v}},\bm{\mathrm{t}}_{k}))/\tau)}{\sum^{K}_{k^{\prime}=1}\exp(\text{cos}(\bm{\mathrm{v}},\bm{\mathrm{t}}_{k^{\prime}}))/\tau)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{cos}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{cos}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> as a similarity function, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> as the temperature parameter and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[;]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo>;</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[;]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes concatenation. More details on how <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{t}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐭</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{t}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed is provided in Supp. Mat.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the cross-entropy loss, we employ a regularization constraint to ensure consistency between two augmented views of an image. Thus, the consistency loss <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{con}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>con</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{con}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{con}}=1-\text{cos}(\mathcal{E}_{v}(\bm{\mathrm{I}}),\mathcal{E}_{v}(\bm{\mathrm{x}}^{\prime}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>con</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>cos</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{con}}=1-\text{cos}(\mathcal{E}_{v}(\bm{\mathrm{I}}),\mathcal{E}_{v}(\bm{\mathrm{x}}^{\prime})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{x}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{x}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the augmented version of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, the total loss function is given by:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{ce}}+\alpha\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{con}},\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ce</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>con</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{ce}}+\alpha\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{con}},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a balancing coefficient for the regularization term, ensuring both alignment and consistency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.3 </span>Client-side training and server aggregation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our approach, multiple remote clients participate in distinct image classification tasks, collaboratively training the PromptFormer network, parameterized by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\{\Theta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\{\Theta\}</annotation></semantics></math>. At each communication round the client transmits <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> to the server, which is then aggregated at the server. Through training on a diverse set of classification tasks distributed across clients, and aggregating the parameters at the server, the resulting model achieves generalization capability to classes and domains unseen during training. We have provided the detailed pseudo-code of our FedMVP algorithm in Supp. Mat.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Initially, the server randomly initializes the parameters <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and at each communication round only a random subset of remote clients <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> will be chosen by the server for sharing the updated parameters <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. Each communication round <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> involves the following steps:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(i) <span id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model initialization:</span> Each client <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in a selected subset <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> receives the latest model parameters <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> to configure its local model.
<br class="ltx_break">(ii) <span id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Local optimization:</span> Following Eq. <a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.3.2 Training of FedMVP ‣ 3.3 Federated Multimodal Visual Prompt Tuning ‣ 3 Method ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, client <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> optimizes its local parameters <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> using an SGD optimizer over <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> iterations with learning rate <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\rho^{r+1}_{i}=\texttt{SGD}_{J}(\eta^{r},\rho^{r},\mathcal{C}_{i},\mathcal{D}_{i}).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">SGD</mtext><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\rho^{r+1}_{i}=\texttt{SGD}_{J}(\eta^{r},\rho^{r},\mathcal{C}_{i},\mathcal{D}_{i}).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">(iii) <span id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter aggregation:</span> After local training, each client <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> transmits its updated parameters <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{r+1}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{r+1}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> back to the server, where they are aggregated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\rho^{r+1}=\frac{1}{|S_{r}|}\sum_{i\in S_{r}}\rho^{r+1}_{i}.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\rho^{r+1}=\frac{1}{|S_{r}|}\sum_{i\in S_{r}}\rho^{r+1}_{i}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The above steps are repeated for <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> communication rounds, after which the server converges on the final model parameters <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho^{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. This iterative process facilitates collaborative and distributed optimization of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> across client data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lightweight fine-tuning in clients</span>. As the FL setup exhibits high data heterogeneity, different clients possess varying numbers of samples. Thus, treating every client as equal and repeated fine-tuning all the parameters, <em id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, can lead to severe overfitting in some clients, especially those with very limited training data. To prevent this, we propose to inject low-rank decomposition matrices, or LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, to the PromptFormer module, and fine-tune these LoRA matrices if the training loss <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}</annotation></semantics></math> in a client starts below a certain loss value <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, which we set to 0.5. In particular, only the LoRA weights corresponding to the query, key and value matrices of the attention layers are trained, while freezing the parameters <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. In that case, the client shares only the LoRA matrices with the server. This not only helps in boosting generalization, but also brings down the communication overhead of clients by around <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 267" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>267</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 267</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we systematically evaluate our proposed FedMVP on three distinct settings that are aimed to gauge the (i) generalization to unseen <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">domains</span> (or Domain Generalization) in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Domain Generalization ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, (ii) generalization to unseen <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">categories</span> (or Base-to-New Generalization) in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Base-to-New Generalization ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, and (iii) generalization to unseen <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">datasets</span> (or Cross-Dataset Generalization) in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Cross-dataset Generalization ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.
Finally, we report the results of our ablation study in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> that are aimed to assess impact of the design choices made in FedMVP.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span>
Following previous FL work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, the base-to-new generalization setting encompasses nine medium-sized image recognition datasets, for base-to-new generalization task. For the domain generalization setting we have used the ImageNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and DomainBed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> benchmarks. In addition, we have also included EuroSAT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> for the cross-dataset generalization setting, alongside all the nine datasets In total, in this work we have used 20 datasets for a comprehensive experimental evaluation. Detailed dataset descriptions and statistics have been reported in the Supp. Mat.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
We have compared our method with the state-of-the-art approaches using CLIP backbone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> that include: zero-shot CLIP (ZS-CLIP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, KgCoOp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, CoCoOp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, and MaPLe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, which were originally designed for offline and non-FL setup, but adapted to the federated setting, as in FedTPG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. Additionally, we have evaluated against CLIP-based methods specifically designed to work in the FL setting, such as FedTPG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, FedOTP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, PromptFL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and FedCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation details.</span> We have employed the frozen CLIP backbone (ViT-B/16) and GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> as the LLM in our experiments. Each MHCA module of <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, consists of a 4-head cross-attention layers, complemented by layer normalization and each FFN comprising of a two-layer bottleneck structure (Linear-GeLU-Linear). The query prompt <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathrm{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathrm{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a length <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=4</annotation></semantics></math> and matches the patch embedding dimension <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}=768" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>768</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}=768</annotation></semantics></math>. The projection layer <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{proj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>proj</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{proj}}</annotation></semantics></math> maps the textual dimension of <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math> to the patch embedding dimension via a linear transformation. In eq. <a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.3.2 Training of FedMVP ‣ 3.3 Federated Multimodal Visual Prompt Tuning ‣ 3 Method ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we keep <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=10</annotation></semantics></math>. We have set the batch size to 128, number of shots per class to 8, and the learning rate equal to 0.003 with a decay rate of <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\text{e-}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>e-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\text{e-}5</annotation></semantics></math> and momentum of 0.9.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Base-to-New Generalization</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental setup.</span> To recap, the base-to-new generalization setting encompasses nine different datasets. The setting is characterized by splitting the classes from each dataset into two groups – base and new. The samples from the base classes are used for training, and the samples from the new classes are held-out to test the generalization performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>. Further, in the non-IID federated setting, the samples of bases classes from all nine datasets are distributed to the clients, such that each client does not see samples from more than 20 disjoint classes (also termed as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">local</span> classes).
We report the model’s classification accuracy on the client’s local classes, all the base classes (spread across clients) and the held-out new classes that were never seen by any client. Note that the server does not train any model and solely conducts parameter aggregation. All the evaluation, except local accuracy, is done at the server.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Base-to-New Generalization setting.<span id="S4.T2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Accuracies (%) on client’s local (seen) classes, base (seen) classes and new (unseen) classes. Harmonic mean (HM) is computed with Base and New class accuracies. Best number in each column is in bold and second-best underlined. Improvement row reports the absolute difference between the best and second-best numbers.</span></span></figcaption>
