</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main results.</span> In Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Base-to-New Generalization ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we report the performance in the base-to-new generalization setting. The results show that the textual and multimodal prompting methods, excel on the seen local and base classes, have at par or poorer generalization on the new classes than the ZS-CLIP model. This hints at potential overfitting by textual prompt tuning methods. In contrast, our proposed FedMVP that is based on a prompt generator, which considers multimodal information, demonstrates superior generalization on both of the seen and unseen classes, achieving the highest HM accuracy. While FedTPG also uses a prompt generator to initialize the text prompts, it does not outperform our FedMVP due to lack of comprehensive visual and attribute information in the prompt tuning process. Nevertheless, we investigate our FedMVP in the non-federated offline setting, and it is able to ourperform others in substantial margin.
Detailed results on all datasets are provided in the Supp. Mat.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.T3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Domain Generalization setting on the DomainBed benchmark.<span id="S4.T3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Accuracies (%) on the unseen target domain(s) for the Multi-source Single-target (MSST) and Single-source Multi-target (SSMT) settings. Best number in each column is in bold and second-best underlined. Improvement row reports the absolute difference between the best and second-best numbers.</span></span></figcaption>
