<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Domain Generalization</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental setup.</span> We conduct experiments under two distinct domain generalization (DG) setups: Multi-source Single-target (MSST) and Single-source Multi-target (SSMT). In the MSST DG setting for a given benchmark, we hold out one domain for measuring generalization and use the rest of the domains for training the clients, following leave-one-domain-out protocol for evaluation. Classes are split among clients under the constraint that each client can see images from one domain only. This setting mirrors a practical scenario where the images taken with a smartphone uniquely reflects the taste of a user or socio-economic biases. In a similar manner, in the SSMT DG setting, one domain is used for training the clients in a distributed manner, while the rest of the domains are used for evaluation. More details on the experimental setup is reported in the Supp. Mat.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main results.</span> In Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.1 Base-to-New Generalization ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we report the performance in the MSST and SSMT DG settings on the DomainBed benchmark. We observe that ZS-CLIP is a strong baseline outperforming all the textual and the multimodal prompting methods, with the exception of FedCLIP and our proposed FedMVP. This suggests that sub-optimal prompt tuning can lead to overfitting on the source training domains, thereby hurting generalization. Our FedMVP owing to the use of attributes learns more transferable representations that generalize better to unseen target domains, and can outperform all the competitor methods in almost all the benchmarks. While, SSMT DG setting is more challenging as the clients are exposed to only a single domain, hindering the ability of models to generalize to multiple unseen domains, each potentially exhibiting unique characteristics or class distributions. Detailed results are in Supp. Mat.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Table. <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.1 Base-to-New Generalization ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we report the results in the SSMT DG setting on the ImageNet benchmark. We observe that FedTPG and FedCoCoOp comes close to our FedMVP in terms of average performance, with FedMVP outperforming all the baselines. In particular, the attributes used in our method results in improved generalization on the ImageNet-Sketch dataset, which contains the sketch version of the ImageNet classes, since the attributes of a semantic concept do not vary between real and sketch images.</p>
</div>
