<td id="S4.T6.6.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">MSST DG</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.6.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">ZS-CLIP</td>
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">74.24</td>
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">70.41</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.6.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S4.T6.6.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">only</span></td>
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">75.94</td>
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">71.85</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.6.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r"><math id="S4.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> + <math id="S4.T6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{con}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>con</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{con}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">76.27</td>
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">72.14</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.6.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r">w/o LoRA</td>
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">77.41</td>
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">72.58</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T6.6.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">FedMVP (Full)</td>
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T6.6.1.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">78.27</span></td>
<td id="S4.T6.6.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T6.6.1.6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">73.02</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Participation Rate of the Clients.</span> In Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we vary the participation rate from 10% to 100% and show how the participation rate of the clients impacts performance on both seen and unseen classes and domains. We can clearly observe that our FedMVP always exhibits a gentle yet positive slope in both the settings, indicating that frequent parameter updates improves generalization. Contrarily, for some competitor methods, such as PromptFL, increased participation leads to saturated performance or negative slope in the plot.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2504.20860v1/participation_bton_new.svg" id="S4.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/480;" width="685" height="480"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Base-to-New</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2504.20860v1/participation_msst_new.svg" id="S4.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/492;" width="685" height="492"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">MSST DG</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Sensitivity to participation rate<span id="S4.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> of the clients, on (a) Base-to-New and (b) MSST DG (DomainBed) settings.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational analysis.</span> In Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we plot the accuracy vs. communication rounds for FedMVP and competitors in base-to-new and MSST settings. While FedMVP transmits 2<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> more parameters than FedTPG, it converges nearly 10<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster, requiring fewer rounds. Even before converging, FedMVP reduces the trainable parameters by 267<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> when the LoRA tuning starts, keeping very less parameters than others except PromptFL. Overall, FedMVP has lower total computational cost w.r.t the others when normalized by the number of communication rounds.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2504.20860v1/communication_bton.svg" id="S4.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/492;" width="685" height="492"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Base-to-New</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2504.20860v1/communication_dn.svg" id="S4.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/480;" width="685" height="480"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">MSST DG</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Computational analysis of the methods<span id="S4.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> on (a) Base-to-New and (b) MSST DG (DomainNet) setting. </span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Size of the clients.</span> In Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we vary the number of classes owned by each client <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\{5,10,20\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\{5,10,20\}</annotation></semantics></math> while keeping the size of the dataset fixed. In other words, decreasing the number of classes in each client results in an increase in the total number of clients participating in the federated setup. We observe that a for all the baselines a higher heterogeneity (or fewer classes per client) leads to lower performance and vice versa. Nevertheless, FedMVP still exhibits a much superior performance when compared to the baselines, outperforming them while having 4x more clients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The additional ablation studies of number of shots, effect of LLMs, sensitivity of <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> hyperparameter and performance in non-federated offline setting are given in Supp. Mat.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2504.20860v1/clientsize_bton_new.svg" id="S4.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/495;" width="685" height="495"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Base-to-New</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2504.20860v1/clientsize_msst_new.svg" id="S4.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/495;" width="685" height="495"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">MSST DG</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Sensitivity to number of classes per client<span id="S4.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> on (a) Base-to-New and (b) MSST DG (DomainNet) setting.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we proposed a novel VLM prompt tuning framework called FedMVP, to address the challenges of unseen domain and class generalization in a centralized FL set up. FedMVP aims to improve the multimodal alignment across multiple clients, via enhancing the visual embedding through textual attribute features. Our findings reveal that FedMVP can effectively learn from heterogeneous clients, boosting its generalizability across domains, as well as outperforming all of the baselines across benchmarks.</p>
</div>
</section>
