<section id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Superiority of FedMVP in non-federated offline setting.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#A2.T19" title="Table 19 ‣ Superiority of FedMVP in non-federated offline setting. ‣ B.5 Detailed ablation studies ‣ Appendix B Additional experimental results ‣ FedMVP: Federated Multi-modal Visual Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>, we present the performance of FedMVP, demonstrating its potential to significantly improve vision-language alignment, even in non-federated offline settings. For comparison, we include a range of state-of-the-art methods, such as zeroshot CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> and several recent non-federated prompt learning techniques, including CoOp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>, CoCoOp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, VPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, KgCoOp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, MaPLe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, PromptSRC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, StyLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, CoPrompt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, TCP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, DePT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, and DeKgTCP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. These methods are evaluated within the base-to-new generalization setting. Our findings reveal that while DePT achieves the highest performance on the base classes, our FedMVP outperforms all the competitors when it comes to unseen new classes, as well as the harmonic mean across both base and new classes. This highlights the robustness of FedMVP, which not only demonstrates a reduced tendency to overfit but also exhibits superior adaptability to unseen samples during inference. These results underscore the effectiveness of FedMVP in generalizing across both known and novel data distributions, making it a promising approach for real-world vision-language tasks.</p>
</div>
